{
  "id": "Compound::DB00741 - Disease::DOID:3310",
  "verbalization": "Hydrocortisone is implicated in the treatment of Atopic Dermatitis through multiple indirect pathways, primarily via its role as a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist. The evidence suggests that Hydrocortisone may act through the corticosteroid receptor pathway, with its effects potentially mediated by compounds like Methylprednisolone or Dexamethasone, both of which are corticosteroids. These steroid compounds are known to modulate inflammatory responses by inhibiting the activity of pro-inflammatory molecules, and their treatment of Atopic Dermatitis supports a mechanism of action involving anti-inflammatory pathways. The lowest common ancestor of Hydrocortisone and these steroid compounds is the 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid, indicating a shared biochemical pathway. While the direct causal link between Hydrocortisone and Atopic Dermatitis is not explicitly stated, the indirect evidence through corticosteroid receptor activation and its downstream effects on inflammation provides a plausible mechanism for the observed relationship.",
